      "_evidence": "In the case of restraint, in the first block, we found differences between genders, F(1,286) = 16.01, p < .001 explaining 5% of the variance (R2Adj = .05). In this case, women reported higher restraint, β = .23, t = 4.00, p < .001. When the sport records was added to the regression (second block), the model accounted for 8% of the variance, F(2,285) = 12.06, p < .001 (R2Adj = .07). Athletes with the best sport records (national champions) reported higher restraint, β = .16, t = 2.78, p < .01. The inclusion of the psychological dimensions in the third block resulted in a significant model, F(6,281) = 19.95, p < .001 accounting for 30% of the variance (R2Adj = .28). Thus, after taking into account the effects of gender and sport records, athletes' impression motivation, satisfaction with body shape and physical appearance, and threat perception emerged as significant predictors. Thus, higher restraint was predicted by higher impression motivation, β = .15, t = 2.75, p < .01 and threat perception, β = .15, t = 2.64, p < .01, and by less satisfaction with body shape and physical appearance, β = -.38, t = -7.38, p < .001.",
